  "name": "Colombia de Monterrey (regiomontano cumbia vallenata)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "From the 1960s the working-class barrios of Monterrey, above all Colonia Independencia, adopted Colombian cumbia and vallenato records as their own, and local accordion groups recreated them for neighbourhood dances and sound-system parties. Celso Piña, known as el rebelde del acordeón, and his Ronda Bogotá became the scene's leading band from the 1980s, and the anthropologist Darío Blanco Arboleda documented the movement as La Colombia de Monterrey.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1960s colombia de monterrey, groove Percussion, Latin am branch. button accordion three row hohner legato acordeon vallenato, vallenato tradition wood metal fork guacharaca, hands layered congas, electric bass. live tracking. Telefunken ELA M 251, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb.",
